# Theory
We will use the Crank-Nicolson method to back work the price of options using the Black Scholes PDE. As a result, we will get a no-arbitrage options price at the current time and be able to create a replicating portfolio under a risk-neutral measure. The Crank-Nicolson method starts by considering the Black Scholes PDE <br>
<center> $\frac{\partial V}{\partial t} = -\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2 S^2 \frac{\partial ^2V}{\partial S^2} - rS \frac{\partial V}{\partial S} + rV   $</center> <br>
and turning it into a finite difference problem as follows <br>
<center> $ \frac{\partial V_i}{\partial t} = -\frac{1}{2}\sigma^2 (i\Delta S^2) \frac{V_{i+1} - 2V_i + V_{i-1}}{\Delta S^2} - r(i\Delta S) \frac{V_{i+1} - V_{i-1}}{2\Delta S} + rV_i.$</center>
<br> The LHS can be evaulted in a similar fashion as <br>
<center>$ \frac{V_i^{n+1} - V_i^n}{\Delta t} = \frac{1}{2} L(V_i^{n+1} + V_i^n) $</center>
<br> where $L$ is the spatial operator as defined in equation (2).
<br>
<br>
The systems can then can evulated and solved as a linear system of equations using matrices, <br>
$A_i = \frac{1}{2} \sigma^2 i^2 - \frac{ri}{2}$ <br>
$B_i = -\sigma^2 i^2 - r$ <br>
$C_i = \frac{1}{2} \sigma^2 i^2 + \frac{ri}{2}$
<br> and similarly for the time-dependent variables, <br>
$\alpha_i = \frac{\Delta A_i}{2}$<br>
$\beta_i = \frac{\Delta B_i}{2}$<br>
$\gamma_i = \frac{\Delta C_i}{2}$<br>
Finally, we can solve the Crank-Nicolson by using the equation <br>
<center>$AV^{n+1} = BV^n + \mathrm{boundary}$ $\mathrm{conditions}$</center> <br>


